s","api_error_status":null,"result":"ETF shares outstanding change only through the **primary market**, where Authorized Participants (APs, typically large broker-dealers) deal directly with the fund.\n\n**Creations (shares increase):**\n- When demand pushes the ETF's price above its NAV, an AP delivers a basket of underlying securities (or cash) to the fund.\n- In exchange, the fund issues new ETF shares in large blocks called **creation units** (often 10,000–50,000 shares).\n- The AP sells these shares on the exchange, adding supply and pulling the price back toward NAV.\n\n**Redemptions (shares decrease):**\n- When the price falls below NAV, an AP buys ETF shares in the market and returns a creation unit to the fund.\n- The fund delivers the underlying basket (or cash) and **cancels** those shares.\n\n**Key point:** Ordinary secondary-market trading between investors doesn't change shares outstanding. Only AP creation and redemption activity does. This arbitrage mechanism keeps prices close to NAV, and in-kind redemptions support the ETF's tax efficiency.","ttft_ms":1112,"type":"result","duration_ms":4196,"uuid":"5dbe6286-be1a-49d3-aa41-1aca8c83f267","ttft_stream_ms":1064,"time_to_request_ms":69,"first_content_frame_ms":1064,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
